even with political divisions across the  country 46 states have joined the  government's groundbreaking legal  challenge to facebook  two antitrust lawsuits accuse the  world's biggest social network of  illegally  shutting down the competition now if  these lawsuits  succeed facebook may have to sell two of  its most popular apps  jeff begays has more on this story  the federal trade commission is asking  for a federal court  to force facebook to sell off assets  like instagram and whatsapp  arguing that in acquiring those  competitors facebook illegally created a  monopoly  today's enforcement action aims to  restore competition to this important  industry  and provide a foundation for future  competitors to grow  and to innovate without the threat of  being crushed by facebook  facebook shot back calling the ftc's  lawsuit  revisionist history saying the  commission cleared these acquisitions  years ago  the government now wants a do-over  sending a chilling warning to american  business that no  sale is ever final but it's not just the  ftc  that has its eye on facebook on  wednesday a bipartisan group of  attorneys  general from nearly every u.s state  filed its own lawsuit against facebook  accusing the company of predatory  behavior  back in july ceo mark zuckerberg  defended facebook's acquisition of  instagram  which it bought for one billion dollars  in 2012.  the ftc had all of these documents and  reviewed this and unanimously voted  at the time not to challenge the  acquisition facebook has acquired about  70 companies over the past 15 years  and since facebook acquired instagram  and whatsapp  both platforms have become far more  popular  and help facebook reach its current  value of nearly  800 billion dollars for cbs this morning  jeff begays in washington
 

